@*
    A tag picker with no JS at all: the popup is a real listbox in the DOM, the
    input owns the focus the whole time, and `aria-activedescendant` moves the
    reading cursor instead of the focus. That is what makes the keyboard route —
    Up/Down to walk the list, Enter to take one, Backspace on an empty input to
    drop the last chip, Escape to close — behave the same for a mouse user and a
    screen reader user.

    An option may name a section it belongs to, and the open list draws those
    sections. Three things about how, because none of them is the obvious choice:

    The section is a real `role="group"` carrying a visible label, not a header row
    dressed up as one. Nothing here ever moves the focus, so a presentational
    heading would be a thing on screen that the keyboard route cannot reach and the
    reading cursor never lands on — a sighted reader would see the boundary and a
    screen reader user would cross it in silence. DataTable states the same
    rationale for its `th scope="colgroup"`: the element that labels the run has to
    be the element that says it does. `aria-labelledby` pointing at the label is
    what makes the group announce its name on the way in.

    Sections are runs, walked off `Matches` in order — the first option whose group
    differs from the one before it opens a new one. Not a `GroupBy`: that would
    coalesce two non-adjacent runs of the same name into one, which is this control
    quietly deciding an ordering. The host owns the order (see SelectorOption.Group),
    and a control that re-derived it would be a second definition of it, free to
    disagree with the first. Walking the matches also means a section whose options
    all fell out of the query takes its heading with it, without anything having to
    check for that case.

    The index an option is identified by stays flat and stays keyed on `Matches`,
    so nesting some options one level deeper changes nothing about `_activeIndex`,
    the cycling arithmetic, or the id `aria-activedescendant` resolves. A section is
    a fact about the markup, not about the order the keyboard walks.

    An option with no group is left where it was, an unwrapped child of the listbox.
    Giving it a heading would mean inventing a name for it, and the only honest name
    is one the host did not supply — so instead a list nobody grouped renders exactly
    the markup it rendered before groups existed.
*@

<div class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId">
    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Label))
    {
        <label class="field__label" for="@InputId">@Label</label>
    }

    <div class="tag-select__control">
        @foreach (var value in VisibleValues)
        {
            <TagChip Text="@LabelFor(value)"
                     Removable="true"
                     Disabled="@Disabled"
                     CssClass="tag-select__chip"
                     OnRemove="() => RemoveAsync(value)" />
        }
        @if (HiddenCount > 0)
        {
            <span class="tag-select__overflow">+@HiddenCount</span>
        }

        <input id="@InputId"
               class="tag-select__input"
               type="text"
               role="combobox"
               autocomplete="off"
               value="@_query"
               placeholder="@Placeholder"
               disabled="@Disabled"
               aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
               aria-controls="@ListId"
               aria-autocomplete="list"
               aria-activedescendant="@ActiveOptionId"
               aria-label="@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Label) ? Placeholder : null)"
               @oninput="OnQueryInput"
               @onfocus="() => _open = true"
               @onblur="OnBlur"
               @onkeydown="OnKeyDownAsync"
               @onkeydown:preventDefault="_preventDefault"
               @attributes="AdditionalAttributes" />
    </div>

    @if (_open && (Matches.Count > 0 || CanCreate))
    {
        var matches = Matches;
        var runs = SectionRuns(matches);

        @* preventDefault on mousedown keeps the focus in the input, so the
           blur that closes the popup cannot fire before this click lands. *@
        RenderFragment<int> option = index =>
            @<li id="@OptionId(index)"
                 class="@OptionClass(index)"
                 role="option"
                 aria-selected="@(index == _activeIndex ? "true" : "false")"
                 @onmousedown:preventDefault="true"
                 @onclick="() => AddAsync(matches[index].Value)"><span class="tag-select__option-label">@matches[index].Label</span>@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(matches[index].Hint))
                 {
                     <span class="tag-select__hint">@matches[index].Hint</span>
                 }</li>;

        <ul id="@ListId" class="tag-select__list" role="listbox" aria-label="@(Label ?? "Tags")">
            @foreach (var run in runs)
            {
                if (run.Name is null)
                {
                    for (var i = run.Start; i < run.End; i++)
                    {
                        @option(i)
                    }
                }
                else
                {
                    var groupId = $"{InputId}-group-{run.Ordinal}";
                    <li class="tag-select__group" role="group" aria-labelledby="@groupId">
                        <span id="@groupId" class="tag-select__group-label">@run.Name</span>
                        <ul class="tag-select__group-list" role="none">
                            @for (var i = run.Start; i < run.End; i++)
                            {
                                @option(i)
                            }
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                }
            }
            @if (CanCreate)
            {
                <li id="@OptionId(Matches.Count)"
                    class="@OptionClass(Matches.Count)"
                    role="option"
                    aria-selected="@(_activeIndex == Matches.Count ? "true" : "false")"
                    @onmousedown:preventDefault="true"
                    @onclick="() => AddAsync(_query.Trim())">@CreateLabel</li>
            }
        </ul>
    }
</div>

@code {
    private readonly string _id = "tag-select-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
    private string _query = string.Empty;
    private bool _open;
    private bool _preventDefault;
    private int _activeIndex = -1;

    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> Options { get; set; } = [];

    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<string> SelectedValues { get; set; } = [];

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<IReadOnlyList<string>> SelectedValuesChanged { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Label { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Placeholder { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Lets the user commit whatever they typed as a new tag.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool AllowCreate { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How many chips fit before the rest collapse into a "+n" count.
    /// Zero or less shows them all.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int MaxVisible { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Id { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    private string InputId => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Id) ? _id : Id;

    private string ListId => InputId + "-list";

    private string OptionId(int index) => $"{InputId}-option-{index}";

    private string ClassValue
    {
        get
        {
            var open = _open ? " tag-select--open" : string.Empty;
            var extra = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? string.Empty : " " + CssClass;
            return $"field tag-select{open}{extra}";
        }
    }

    private IReadOnlyList<string> VisibleValues =>
        MaxVisible > 0 && SelectedValues.Count > MaxVisible
            ? SelectedValues.Take(MaxVisible).ToList()
            : SelectedValues;

    private int HiddenCount => MaxVisible > 0 ? Math.Max(0, SelectedValues.Count - MaxVisible) : 0;

    private List<SelectorOption> Matches => Options
        .Where(option => !SelectedValues.Contains(option.Value, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
        .Where(option => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_query)
            || option.Label.Contains(_query.Trim(), StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
        .ToList();

    private bool CanCreate =>
        AllowCreate
        && !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_query)
        && !SelectedValues.Contains(_query.Trim(), StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
        && !Options.Any(option => string.Equals(option.Value, _query.Trim(), StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));

    private int OptionCount => Matches.Count + (CanCreate ? 1 : 0);

    private string CreateLabel => $"Add \"{_query.Trim()}\"";

    private string? ActiveOptionId => _open && _activeIndex >= 0 && _activeIndex < OptionCount
        ? OptionId(_activeIndex)
        : null;

    /// <summary>One stretch of <c>Matches</c> that renders together: a named
    /// section, or the unwrapped options between two of them. <c>Ordinal</c>
    /// numbers only the named ones, because it exists to name the label element a
    /// section points <c>aria-labelledby</c> at.</summary>
    private readonly record struct OptionRun(string? Name, int Ordinal, int Start, int End);

    private static List<OptionRun> SectionRuns(IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> matches)
    {
        var runs = new List<OptionRun>();
        var ordinal = 0;

        for (var index = 0; index < matches.Count;)
        {
            var name = matches[index].Group;
            var start = index;

            while (index < matches.Count && string.Equals(matches[index].Group, name, StringComparison.Ordinal))
            {
                index++;
            }

            runs.Add(new OptionRun(name, name is null ? -1 : ordinal++, start, index));
        }

        return runs;
    }

    private string OptionClass(int index) => index == _activeIndex
        ? "tag-select__option tag-select__option--active"
        : "tag-select__option";

    private string LabelFor(string value) =>
        Options.FirstOrDefault(option => string.Equals(option.Value, value, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))?.Label
        ?? value;

    private void OnQueryInput(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        _query = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty;
        _open = true;
        _activeIndex = OptionCount > 0 ? 0 : -1;
    }

    private async Task OnKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        // Arrow keys would scroll the popup's ancestor and Enter would submit a
        // surrounding form, so those are the keys we swallow — and only those.
        _preventDefault = e.Key is "ArrowDown" or "ArrowUp" or "Enter" or "Escape";

        switch (e.Key)
        {
            case "ArrowDown":
                _open = true;
                if (OptionCount > 0) _activeIndex = (_activeIndex + 1) % OptionCount;
                break;

            case "ArrowUp":
                _open = true;
                if (OptionCount > 0) _activeIndex = (_activeIndex <= 0 ? OptionCount : _activeIndex) - 1;
                break;

            case "Enter":
                if (!_open || _activeIndex < 0 || _activeIndex >= OptionCount) break;
                await AddAsync(_activeIndex < Matches.Count ? Matches[_activeIndex].Value : _query.Trim());
                break;

            case "Escape":
                _open = false;
                _activeIndex = -1;
                break;

            case "Backspace":
                if (_query.Length > 0 || SelectedValues.Count == 0) break;
                await RemoveAsync(SelectedValues[^1]);
                break;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Closes the popup when focus genuinely leaves the control.
    ///
    /// There was no way to dismiss it with a mouse at all: the list opened on
    /// focus, stayed open after a pick, and only Escape closed it — so it sat
    /// over the page covering whatever was underneath. Options preventDefault
    /// their mousedown, so this only fires for a click outside or a Tab away.
    /// </summary>
    private void OnBlur()
    {
        _open = false;
        _activeIndex = -1;
    }

    private async Task AddAsync(string value)
    {
        if (Disabled || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value)) return;
        if (SelectedValues.Contains(value, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) return;

        var next = SelectedValues.ToList();
        next.Add(value);

        _query = string.Empty;
        _activeIndex = -1;
        // Taking an option answers the question the popup was asking. Typing or
        // ArrowDown reopens it, so picking several in a row still costs one key.
        _open = false;

        await CommitAsync(next);
    }

    private async Task RemoveAsync(string value)
    {
        if (Disabled) return;

        var next = SelectedValues
            .Where(existing => !string.Equals(existing, value, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            .ToList();

        if (next.Count == SelectedValues.Count) return;

        await CommitAsync(next);
    }

    private Task CommitAsync(List<string> next)
    {
        SelectedValues = next;
        return SelectedValuesChanged.InvokeAsync(next);
    }
}
